{
  "image_name": "0f72c6643d7918f828004f366ef1d3b1.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes several ways nucleic acid binders or related compounds can control gene expression at the RNA level. Across the panels, the common idea is that targeting untranslated regions or stop-codon contexts can redirect translation or RNA processing to increase production of a desired full-length protein.\n\nOne mechanism involves upstream open reading frames in the 5′ UTR. Normally, ribosomes translate these short uORFs and this limits translation of the main coding region. When an NBT binds near the uORF, it appears to block that inhibitory translation, allowing the ribosome to continue into the main protein-coding sequence and produce more protein.\n\nA second strategy targets other regulatory RNA secondary structures in UTRs. Binding seems to disrupt structures or factor-binding sites that interfere with ribosome loading or scanning, again promoting productive translation.\n\nAnother panel shows regulation of polyadenylation. By blocking an early polyadenylation signal, the binder shifts RNA processing away from premature cleavage and toward a downstream site, generating a longer transcript that includes the full coding region.\n\nThe final panel shows small molecules promoting readthrough of a premature termination codon. Instead of stopping and producing a truncated protein, the ribosome incorporates an amino acid and continues translation, restoring a functional protein.",
